<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/advanced/resource-provision-food-energy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Food and energy are provision geography's twin subjects, and they share one structure:
    <strong>supply is unevenly distributed by geology and climate, demand is set by population
    and development, and the mismatches travel — as trade, as prices, as migrants and as
    conflicts.</strong> The world grows enough calories for everyone and generates enough
    electricity to light every home, yet around 735 million people are undernourished and
    hundreds of millions more lack reliable power. That paradox — abundance existing, access
    failing — is the intellectual heart of this unit, and the reason the syllabus keeps asking
    not only "how is it produced?" but "who gets it, and at what cost?". Watch both resources
    move through the same sequence: how production works, where the surpluses and deficits sit,
    what disrupts them, and what the production itself costs.
  </p>

  <h2>How food is produced</h2>
  <p>
    Farming types (syllabus 10.1.1) run on one axis: who eats the output.
    <strong>Subsistence</strong> farming feeds the family first — monsoon rice paddies in
    Bangladesh; <strong>commercial</strong> farming sells into markets — Kansas wheat or
    Brazilian soy. Within that, <strong>arable</strong> (crops), <strong>pastoral</strong>
    (animals) and <strong>mixed</strong> describe the product; and at the high-tech edge,
    <strong>hydroponics, aeroponics and aquaponics</strong> grow food without soil at all —
    the Netherlands, smaller than Scotland, is the world's second-largest agricultural
    exporter by value. Every farm is a <strong>system</strong> (10.1.2): inputs (labour,
    seed, water, fertiliser, machinery), processes (ploughing, sowing, irrigating,
    harvesting), outputs (grain, milk, meat — or little surplus at all, which is the
    subsistence trap). Intensity is the other axis: <strong>intensive</strong> farming
    (high inputs per hectare — rice paddies yielding three crops a year, battery poultry)
    contrasts with <strong>extensive</strong> farming (low inputs spread over vast areas —
    Australian ranching, Canadian prairie wheat). Geography decides the ceiling: soils,
    relief (terracing turns slopes like Banaue's into fields), growing-season temperatures
    and, above all, water — which is why irrigation is the quiet giant of the story.
  </p>

  <h2>The global patterns of food supply and demand</h2>
  <p>
    The pattern (10.2.1) is stark: North America and Europe eat roughly 3,400–3,700 kcal per
    person per day; Sub-Saharan Africa averages around 2,400–2,500. Yet the world grows enough
    for everyone — the calculator below shows it. Global cereal output is about 2.8 Gt a year
    from ~710 million hectares at ~4 t/ha (FAO figures, rounded), which is ~355 kg of cereal
    per person per year — over 3,000 kcal a day <em>if it reached a human mouth directly</em>
    instead of animal feed, fuel tanks and landfills. It doesn't: FAO's 2023 report counts
    ~735 million undernourished people and ~2.4 billion moderately or severely food insecure.
    The geographer's insight, from economist Amartya Sen, is that most hunger is a failure of
    <em>access</em> (poverty, conflict, distribution), not of global supply — roughly a third
    of food is lost or wasted between field and plate, lost mostly in storage and transport in
    poorer countries and wasted on plates in richer ones. Globalisation of food (10.2.4, 10.2.5)
    delivers strawberries in December and transmits shocks with equal efficiency: when war hit
    the Black Sea breadbasket in 2022, prices jumped in Cairo and Nairobi, because wheat-importing
    countries had optimised for cheapness rather than resilience.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the world is hungry because it does not grow enough food."
    The arithmetic says otherwise — the planet's croplands already supply well over 2,800 kcal
    per person per day, before counting livestock and fisheries. Hunger clusters where poverty,
    war and broken infrastructure block access, and where export crops crowd out subsistence
    ones; adding global output without changing who can buy food would leave most of the
    hungry exactly where they are. This is why food-security policy is as much about incomes,
    storage and peace as about agronomy.
  </p>

  <h2>The challenges of food supply</h2>
  <p>
    The challenges (10.3) stack on three fronts. <strong>Raising supply</strong> (10.3.3):
    irrigation is the biggest single lever — FAO reckons the roughly 20 per cent of cultivated
    land that is irrigated produces around 40 per cent of the harvest — alongside new seed
    varieties (the Green Revolution's dwarf wheats and rices, which Borlaug's programme spread
    from Mexico in the 1940s–60s and which multiplied Asian yields), fertiliser, better
    storage, and land reclamation such as the Great Green Wall of trees being planted across
    the Sahel to push back desertification. But each lever has a bill: irrigation salinises
    soil when drainage is poor, and the Green Revolution's fertiliser and pesticide dependence
    favoured farmers who could afford inputs. <strong>Food aid</strong> (10.3.4) saves lives in
    acute famine — Ethiopia 1984–85, where international relief moved after a BBC report
    shocked the world — but can undercut local farmers when free grain floods the same market:
    the modern response is to buy locally wherever possible. <strong>Security against shocks</strong>
    is the newest lesson: monoculture is the standing risk (Ireland's potato blight, 1845, is
    the syllabus's eternal example — one crop, one pathogen, one million dead), and
    concentration is the modern one, with Russia and Ukraine normally around a quarter of
    world wheat exports between them. A resilient food system diversifies crops, sources and
    suppliers, and keeps buffer stocks — which is why the 2008 price crisis, when export bans
    and panic buying stacked a price spike into riots in dozens of countries, rewrote how
    governments think about grain.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the world's calorie budget: drag population and yields — supply per person stays comfortable on paper, which is exactly why hunger is a distribution problem" widget="sliderLab" config={foodCfg} />

  <h2>How our energy is produced</h2>
  <p>
    Energy types (10.4.1) split into <strong>non-renewables</strong> — coal, oil, natural gas,
    and nuclear (uranium fuel is mined, so nuclear counts as non-renewable even though its
    carbon emissions are low) — and <strong>renewables</strong>: hydroelectric, wind, solar,
    tidal, wave, geothermal and biomass. Fuelwood sits in a class of its own: renewable only
    if regrown faster than cut, which in much of the Sahel it is not. The production route
    differs by source: fossil plants burn fuel to boil water and drive turbines; hydro
    converts falling water; solar panels convert light directly; wind converts moving air.
    The engineering trade-off that shapes every national grid is <strong>firm versus
    intermittent</strong>: coal, gas and nuclear run on schedule whatever the weather, while
    wind and solar produce only when the resource is there — which is why grids pair them
    with storage, interconnection and flexible back-up (gas turbines that can ramp quickly),
    and why the transition is an engineering project, not just an installation one. Where
    each source is used depends on endowment: Iceland sits on a volcanic hotspot (hydro and
    geothermal supply nearly all its electricity), the Gulf sits on oil and gas, Denmark on
    shallow windy seas.
  </p>

  <h2>The global patterns of energy supply and demand</h2>
  <p>
    Demand keeps rising (10.5.1) with population and industrialisation — roughly a billion
    people still lack reliable electricity — but the supply mix is shifting unevenly: fig. 2
    shows electricity, the fastest-moving sector. Coal-plus-gas slid from ~62% to ~58% of world
    generation between 2010 and 2023, hydro holds near 14–16%, nuclear sagged after Fukushima,
    and wind plus solar climbed from ~2% to over 13% (Ember, rounded). Where surpluses and
    deficits lie (10.5.2) is mostly geology and luck: the Middle East holds around half the
    world's proven oil reserves; Russia built its leverage on gas pipelines; Japan, with
    almost none of either, imports nearly all its energy, so <strong>energy security</strong> —
    reliable, affordable supply — is existential there, driving both its nuclear programme and
    its post-Fukushima gas dependence. Dependence is measurable and dangerous: before 2022,
    Russia supplied around 40% of Europe's imported gas, and the war in Ukraine turned that
    dependency into a strategic wound — Europe paid four times historic gas prices, raced to
    build terminals for liquefied natural gas from the US and Qatar, and shortened its energy
    transition timetable in one winter. Mixes also differ by development level (10.5.3):
    firewood and charcoal still dominate household energy across much of Sub-Saharan Africa
    (with deforestation and indoor smoke as the costs), middle-income industrialisers lean on
    coal for cheap scale, and high-income countries buy efficiency and diversification —
    the energy ladder tracks the income ladder.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="world electricity by source, 2010 to 2023 (shares in %, rounded, Ember) — watch solar's slope against the fossil plateau" widget="plotter" config={energyCfg} />

  <h2>The impacts of energy production</h2>
  <p>
    Every source has a bill (10.6.1). Counted over its whole life, coal emits ~820 g of CO₂
    per kWh, gas ~490, solar ~45 and wind ~11 (IPCC medians). Land matters too: nuclear is
    compact but leaves waste for millennia; wind farms occupy wide areas yet the farming goes
    on between turbines; dams flood valleys — China's Three Gorges displaced over a million
    people — and reservoirs emit methane. Extraction scars: mountaintop coal, oil spills
    (Exxon Valdez 1989, Deepwater Horizon 2010), and around 40% of the US corn crop going
    into ethanol, competing with plates. The named example (10.6.4) is <strong>Brazil</strong>:
    its electricity is about 60% hydro, transport runs heavily on sugarcane ethanol (~30
    billion litres a year, a programme begun with Proálcool in 1975), and most new cars are
    flex-fuel — yet Brazil is also a major offshore oil exporter, and dams and ethanol push
    against the rainforest: the same cane fields that displace soy and cattle ranching into
    forest frontier. Impacts are also distributed unfairly: emissions concentrate where
    energy is burned, extraction damage where it is mined, and the poorest households —
    those least responsible — pay the highest share of income for fuel and suffer the worst
    air. Managing supplies sustainably (10.6.2, 10.6.3) means stacking strategies:
    efficiency first (the cheapest barrel and kilowatt-hour are the ones never needed),
    diversified renewables for security, storage and grids to firm them up, and honest
    pricing of the emissions. The 1973 shock taught the lesson once; the 2022 spike set it
    as homework.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "hydroelectric power is clean, so it is impact-free." The
    carbon arithmetic is favourable, but dams are among the most physically invasive works
    humans build: valleys flooded and communities displaced (Three Gorges: over a million
    people), fish migration blocked (the Mekong's fisheries), sediment starvation downstream,
    and methane bubbling from flooded vegetation in tropical reservoirs. "Renewable" describes
    the fuel, not the footprint — a distinction that recurs for biomass (which can drive
    deforestation) and biofuels (which compete with food), and which is why every energy
    decision is a trade-off rather than a solution.
  </p>

  <Widget no="3" caption="food and energy are one story of provision: blight to breadbasket war, embargo to renewables — the shocks that rewrote supply" widget="timeline" config={resTimeline} />

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The food–energy–water nexus.</strong> The two syllabus halves are one system:
    food production consumes around 30% of the world's final energy (fertiliser synthesis,
    irrigation pumping, tractors, refrigeration), energy production consumes water and land
    (biofuels and hydropower compete with crops), and water constrains both. The 2007–08
    food-price crisis was partly an energy event — oil above $100 made biofuels attractive,
    biofuels pulled US corn out of the feed market, and grain prices followed — a textbook
    nexus cascade. Analysts now model all three together, because optimising one at a time
    is how the cascade happens.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Technology curves and the transition's arithmetic.</strong> The cost of solar
    electricity has fallen roughly 90% since 2010 and wind by more than half — the steepest
    learning curves in industrial history, driven by manufacturing scale (and Chinese
    production) rather than laboratory breakthroughs. That changes the geography of energy:
    the resources are now "resource quality times installed cost", so the sunniest and
    windiest developing countries may leapfrog the grid architectures of the twentieth
    century, as some leapt past landline telephony. The unsolved problems are the last
    proportion: firming a grid through a windless, sunless fortnight, decarbonising cement,
    steel, shipping and aviation, and building the transmission lines — permits, not
    turbines, are the new bottleneck in most of Europe and North America.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Demand's other half.</strong> Supply dominates the syllabus, but the demand side
    is where the leverage hides: dietary transition toward meat multiplies land and emissions
    (a kilogram of beef embodies many times the feed calories it returns), food waste at
    retail and household level in rich countries is the equivalent of a large extra harvest,
    and efficiency standards have quietly done as much for energy security as any power
    station.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "food miles are the biggest piece of food's carbon
    footprint." Transport is usually a minor share of a food's lifecycle emissions; what the
    food <em>is</em> matters far more than how far it travelled — production, land-use change
    and refrigeration dominate, and red meat carries a footprint many times that of most
    plant foods regardless of origin. Seasonality and production method beat proximity more
    often than not, which is why "eat less meat, waste less, buy what grows well where you
    are" outperforms "buy local" as climate arithmetic.
  </p>
  <p>
    The frontier question for provision geography this century is blunt: can the
    world deliver an additional ~2 billion people a climate-stable diet and clean energy
    without expanding the cultivated and built area — or does provision finally become,
    like 1845 and 2022, a story about choices rather than endowments?
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Geography 0460 syllabus (2027–2029), topic 10; FAO, The State of Food Security and Nutrition in the World 2023 and FAOSTAT; Ember Global Electricity Review 2024; IPCC lifecycle emissions medians (AR5 Annex III); EPE/UNICA Brazil energy data.</p>
